      <div type="section" xml:id="tei003-spurgeon-primary-references-selection"><quote xml:id="spurgeon-mtp-1-quote-1" type="primary-reference">
          <p xml:id="spurgeon-mtp-1-quote-1-p-1">“I am the Lord, I change not; therefore you sons of Jacob are not consumed.”</p>
          <p xml:id="spurgeon-mtp-1-quote-1-p-2">
            <ref type="scripture" cRef="Mal.3.6">Malachi 3:6</ref>
          </p>
        </quote>
        <list xml:id="spurgeon-mtp-1-list-1" type="ordered" rend="a">
          <item xml:id="spurgeon-mtp-1-list-1-item-1">
            <p xml:id="spurgeon-mtp-1-list-1-item-1-p">First of all, we have set before us the doctrine of THE IMMUTABILITY OF GOD. “I am God, I change not.” Here I shall attempt to expound, or rather to enlarge the thought and then afterwards to bring a few arguments to prove its truth.</p>
          </item>
        </list>
        <quote xml:id="spurgeon-mtp-15-quote-1" type="primary-reference">
          <p xml:id="spurgeon-mtp-15-quote-1-p-1">“I have written to him the great things of My Law, but they were counted as a strange thing.”</p>
          <p xml:id="spurgeon-mtp-15-quote-1-p-2">
            <ref type="scripture" cRef="Hos.8.12">Hosea 8:12</ref>
          </p>
        </quote>
        </div>
